# Accepted ruleset-drift entries (issue #916).
#
# governance-drift.yml's ruleset-drift job diffs each committed
# .github/rulesets/*.json against its live GitHub counterpart and FAILS the
# job when they differ, or when a committed ruleset has no live counterpart
# at all. That is a deliberate alarm (#916 / #502 finding F4 -- this used to
# be advisory-only and never actually failed), not a suggestion -- but
# reconciling a ruleset genuinely takes two separate actions (commit the JSON
# change, then `gh api --method PUT` it live, or the other order) that will
# not always land in the same PR. An entry here is how a maintainer accepts a
# specific, understood, temporary gap between committed and live instead of
# leaving the job red with no explanation.
#
# Same ignore-list-with-justification shape as .trivyignore, .grype.yml,
# docs/security/citation-drift.ignore and docs/security/crypto-surface.ignore:
# an entry is a written-down decision, not a mute button. A ruleset that has
# drifted (or was never applied) and is NOT listed here FAILS the job, and an
# entry here for a ruleset that is back in sync with its committed JSON ALSO
# fails -- so this file cannot quietly accumulate dead suppressions.
#
# Format:  <ruleset name, the committed .github/rulesets/*.json "name" field>  # why the gap exists and how/when it will be resolved
#
# No entries currently accepted -- every committed ruleset is expected to
# match its live counterpart.
